d  disinformation  might be a huge threat to the very idea  of free and open discourse  it doesn't take a lot of dot connecting  to see how our methods for fighting  those things can end up haunting us  it might just end up seeding power to  people in capitol hill or silicon valley  to decide what ideas are too dangerous  it's the same rationale that got shank  thrown in jail under wilson  and that blacklisted actors for their  political affiliations during the  mccarthy era in the 1940s and 50s  the federal government thought they had  a solution to a similar problem the fcc  mandated that tv and radio stations gave  air time to both sides of any  controversial issue  it was called the fairness doctrine but  something happened in the early 1960s  john f kennedy figured out he could game  the doctrine by appointing political  allies to the fcc  and then lean on them to find ways to  manipulate news coverage  he told his buddies to rigorously  enforce the fairness doctrine and they  enforced it to be fair  to kennedy by using their fcc powers to  call out any unbalanced  criticisms of him nixon would later do  the same thing  using the fairness doctrine to pressure  tv networks to give more fair and  balanced coverage of the vietnam war  what he really meant was more flattering  coverage and censorship of the war's  atrocities  okay but what does all of this have to  do with big daddy twitter hiding tweets  and  youtube demonetizing videos well a lot  of people are worried that the same  thing the jfk did to the tv networks  could happen with the internet if the  fcc is in the pocket of whoever's in  charge  they might abuse their power and  influence the media we consume  attempting to coerce internet platforms  into widely skewing content moderation  to what governments want seems  maybe bad and also just like another  kind of censorship  ironically pushes by conservative  politicians to be fair to right wing  news outlets ended up with facebook  systematically favoring right wing news  while suppressing left-wing news  facebook brings us to the central  problem with a lot of censorship  discourse  so much of the history of censorship  relates to the government and the  distribution of the press and public  spaces  problem is public space has virtually  disappeared in the last century in lieu  of public squares and meeting places we  have  shopping malls and privately owned  internet companies and  like the taco bell that threw me out  private companies are in no way  obligated to honor free speech laws but  the effect is the same it's just that  instead of the government regulating  speech  companies do censorship today happens in  a myriad of ways  but our narrow focus can often obscure  the greater systems at play  it's easy to get mad when a professor  loses their job it's harder to look at  everyone who never got a job offer and  why  it's easy to get mad at twitter it's  harder to look around and figure out  what kind of public venues for free  speech still  exist and why they've almost all  disappeared and while some may propose a  free market of ideas to duke it out  the free market has always been a  fiction ideas are filtered through so  many levels of market and constitutive  censorship before they can even get  written in a book or put  on the news while we should be on the  lookout for the socrates drinking  hemlock version of censorship  it's just a small part of the picture  it's an incredibly complicated problem  with no  easy solutions and for everyone who's  saying it is maybe take what they say  with a grain of salt  i'll end with scholar michael hulquist  who writes that censorship is impossible  to escape  and that the institutions or legislation  promising free speech  are band-aid solutions he says to be for  or against  censorship as such is to assume a  freedom no one has  censorship is one can only discriminate  among its more and less repressive  effects  so what do you think is censorship  inevitable or should my lifetime ban a  taco bell be  immediately lifted big thanks to our  patrons for all your support  murder that subscribe button like you're  an athenian condemning socrates  and don't forget to ring that bell and  as always  thanks for watching later  [Applause]  [Music]  so  you


